<body>

<div class="cover">
  <h1>{{ tool_name }}</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Google Ads API — Design Document for Basic Access Application</div>
  <div class="meta">
    <div><strong>Applicant:</strong> {{ applicant_legal_entity }}</div>
    <div><strong>Manager Account:</strong> {{ manager_account_id }}{% if manager_account_name %} ({{ manager_account_name }}){% endif %}</div>
    <div><strong>Tool name:</strong> {{ tool_name }}</div>
    <div><strong>Source repository:</strong> {{ source_repo_url }}</div>
    <div><strong>Contact:</strong> {{ contact_email }}</div>
    <div><strong>Document date:</strong> {{ document_date }}</div>
    <div><strong>{{ tool_name }} version:</strong> {{ bidsmith_version }}</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>1. Executive summary</h2>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} is an internal, open-source command-line tool that lets {{ applicant_legal_entity }}
manage its own Google Ads accounts declaratively from version-controlled <code>.bid</code> files.
It is a &ldquo;Terraform for Google Ads&rdquo;: campaigns, ad groups, ads, keywords, budgets, and
conversion actions are described in HCL2 source files; a <code>plan</code> step diffs that
declaration against the live account using <code>validateOnly=true</code> mutates; an interactive
<code>apply</code> step prints the diff, prompts the operator for explicit confirmation, and only
then issues a real mutate.
</p>

<p>
The tool is used <strong>only by {{ applicant_legal_entity }} to manage its own Google Ads
accounts</strong>. It is not multi-tenant, does not authenticate end users, is not sold, and has
no UI for outside users. End-user data is never read, written, or stored.
</p>

<h2>2. Background, scope, and intended users</h2>

<h3>2.1 Why this tool exists</h3>

{% for paragraph in why_this_tool_exists_paragraphs %}<p>{{ paragraph }}</p>
{% endfor %}

<h3>2.2 Who uses it</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Operators</strong> — {{ who_uses_it_operators }}</li>
  <li><strong>Reviewers</strong> — anyone with read access to the {{ tool_name }} Git repository.
      Changes to <code>.bid</code> files are reviewed in pull requests the same way as
      application code.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3 Who does <em>not</em> use it</h3>

<ul>
  <li>End users / consumers of {{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s products. {{ tool_name }} does
      not authenticate any end-user identity and has no public-facing interface.</li>
  <li>Third-party advertisers. The tool only touches Google Ads customers under
      {{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s manager account. The OAuth credentials it carries are
      {{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s own; there is no &ldquo;manage someone else&rsquo;s
      account&rdquo; feature.</li>
  <li>Automated systems making spend changes without human approval. Every <code>apply</code>
      requires an interactive <code>yes</code> on a TTY, or an explicit <code>--auto-approve</code>
      flag in a controlled CI context.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. System architecture</h2>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} is a single statically linked Rust binary (~4&nbsp;MB on macOS / Linux). It has
no server component, no background daemons, and no persistent local state beyond the
<code>.bid</code> files the operator edits and a small on-disk cache of the most recent live-state
fetch (used to make <code>plan</code> fast; never includes credentials).
</p>

<div class="ascii">.bid files (HCL2)
   |
   v
parser     -- hcl-edit AST, source spans preserved
   |
   v
schema     -- typed resource graph, references resolved
   |
   v
import     -- AST -> in-memory ExportInput  (deterministic, round-trips with renderer)
   |
   v
live state -- googleAds:searchStream  (one read query per resource type)
   |
   v
diff       -- declared vs. live, name-matched, scalar field-level
   |
   v
plan       -- googleAds:mutate  with validateOnly=true   (no writes)
   |
   v
apply      -- print diff -> prompt 'yes' -> googleAds:mutate with validateOnly=false</div>

<p class="note">
The boundary between read-only (everything down to and including <em>plan</em>) and write
(only <em>apply</em>, only after explicit human confirmation) is the central safety property of
the design.
</p>

<h3>3.1 Components</h3>

<table>
  <tr><th>Module</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
  {% for m in modules %}<tr><td><code>{{ m.path }}</code></td><td>{{ m.description }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>

<h2>4. Google Ads API usage</h2>

<h3>4.1 Transport and endpoints</h3>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} uses the <strong>Google Ads REST API</strong> over HTTPS. The gRPC transport is
intentionally <em>not</em> used — REST gives us curl-able endpoints, human-readable wire traffic
for debugging, and no proto-compile step in the build. Endpoint version is pinned via the
<code>BIDSMITH_API_VERSION</code> environment variable (currently <code>{{ api_version }}</code>).
</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Method</th><th>Used by</th><th>validateOnly</th></tr>
  {% for e in endpoints %}<tr><td><code>{{ e.endpoint }}</code></td><td>{{ e.method }}</td><td>{{ e.used_by }}</td><td>{{ e.validate_only }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>

<h3>4.2 Read patterns (GAQL)</h3>

<p>
Reads are issued via <code>googleAds:searchStream</code> with hand-written GAQL queries — one per
resource type {{ tool_name }} models. There are {{ gaql_query_count }} fixed queries
({% for q in gaql_queries %}<code>{{ q.label }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}).
The <code>query</code> command additionally exposes a read-only GAQL passthrough for ad-hoc
inspection.
</p>

<h3>4.3 Write patterns (mutate)</h3>

<p>
All writes go through <code>googleAds:mutate</code> in a single atomic batch per
<code>apply</code> invocation. Operations are ordered by resource dependencies so each
resource&rsquo;s parent exists before children are created. The exact same request body is sent
twice in an interactive <code>apply</code>:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>First with <code>validateOnly: true</code> — Google Ads validates auth, references, enums,
      policy strings, and field-length constraints, but no resource is mutated. The result is
      shown to the operator as the plan.</li>
  <li>If, and only if, the operator types <code>yes</code> at the prompt, the same body is
      re-sent with <code>validateOnly: false</code>. If validateOnly rejected anything, the real
      mutate is <strong>skipped</strong>.</li>
</ol>

<h3>4.4 Frequency and volume</h3>

<table>
  <tr><th>Workload</th><th>Estimated calls/day</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Interactive <code>plan</code> while authoring (peak)</td><td>~50 read + ~10 validateOnly mutate</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Real <code>apply</code> (post-approval)</td><td>~1–5 mutate batches, one per landed PR</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Read-only <code>query</code> for inspection</td><td>~20</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Typical day</strong></td><td><strong>{{ volume_typical_per_day }}</strong></td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Hard ceiling we expect to reach</strong></td><td><strong>{{ volume_ceiling }}</strong></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5. User workflow</h2>

<p>
Every interaction with the Google Ads API is initiated by a human operator running a CLI command
on their own workstation. There is no scheduling, no webhook, no automated trigger.
</p>

<h3>5.1 Typical edit cycle</h3>

<pre><code># 1. Operator edits a .bid file locally
$ vim campaigns/eu_search.bid

# 2. Local-only validation (no API call)
$ {{ tool_name }} validate .
OK: 4 file(s) valid.

# 3. Plan: fetch live state + validateOnly mutate, show the diff
$ {{ tool_name }} plan .
+ create  google_ads_campaign.summer_eu       (validateOnly: ok)
~ update  google_ads_campaign.spring_eu  (status: ENABLED &rarr; PAUSED)  (ok)
  no-op   95 resources unchanged
Plan: 1 to create, 1 to update, 95 unchanged.

# 4. Apply: print the same diff, prompt, then mutate
$ {{ tool_name }} apply .
+ create  google_ads_campaign.summer_eu       (validateOnly: ok)
~ update  google_ads_campaign.spring_eu  (status: ENABLED &rarr; PAUSED)  (ok)

Apply these changes? Only 'yes' will be accepted to approve.
&gt; yes
Applied: 1 created, 1 updated.</code></pre>

<h3>5.2 Refresh / read-back</h3>

<p>
Operators can also run <code>{{ tool_name }} refresh</code> to pull live account state back into
<code>.bid</code> files (read-only, never mutates), <code>{{ tool_name }} pull</code> to dump live
state as raw JSON, and <code>{{ tool_name }} query 'SELECT ... FROM campaign'</code> for ad-hoc
GAQL inspection. All three are read-only paths.
</p>

<h2>6. Required Minimum Functionality (RMF) compliance</h2>

<p>
The Google Ads API RMF requires that any tool with write access exposes a minimum set of
operations to its users. Because {{ tool_name }} is a CLI managing the operator&rsquo;s
<em>own</em> account, the RMF surface is provided through the <code>.bid</code> source files and
the <code>plan</code>/<code>apply</code>/<code>query</code> commands. The mapping:
</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>RMF requirement</th><th>How {{ tool_name }} satisfies it</th></tr>
  {% for r in rmf_table %}<tr><td>{{ r.requirement | safe }}</td><td>{{ r.satisfied_by | safe }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>

<h2>7. Authentication and credential handling</h2>

<h3>7.1 Credentials</h3>

<p>
{{ tool_name }} uses the standard Google Ads API credential set, supplied via environment
variables:
</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GOOGLE_ADS_DEVELOPER_TOKEN</code></td><td>The manager account&rsquo;s developer token.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GOOGLE_ADS_CLIENT_ID</code> / <code>GOOGLE_ADS_CLIENT_SECRET</code></td>
      <td>OAuth 2.0 desktop-app client owned by {{ applicant_legal_entity }}&rsquo;s Google Cloud project.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GOOGLE_ADS_REFRESH_TOKEN</code></td><td>A refresh token issued to a named
          {{ applicant_legal_entity }} employee for the <code>adwords</code> scope.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GOOGLE_ADS_LOGIN_CUSTOMER_ID</code></td><td>The manager (MCC) account ID
          ({{ manager_account_id }}).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>GOOGLE_ADS_CUSTOMER_ID</code></td><td>The specific sub-account to target.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>7.2 Token exchange</h3>

<p>
On every invocation, {{ tool_name }} exchanges the refresh token for a short-lived access token
via <code>https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token</code>. The access token lives only in process
memory for the duration of the run; it is never written to disk and never logged. The refresh
token is the only durable credential, and it lives in the operator&rsquo;s local shell
configuration or a <code>.env</code>-style file outside version control.
</p>

<h3>7.3 Scope</h3>

<p>
The only OAuth scope requested is <code>{{ oauth_scope }}</code>. {{ tool_name }} does not
request, store, or use any other Google scope (no Drive, Gmail, Calendar, Cloud Platform, etc.).
</p>

<h2>8. Data handling</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>What we read from Google Ads:</strong> our own account&rsquo;s configuration —
      campaigns, ad groups, ads, keywords, budgets, conversion actions, assets, and reporting
      metrics for those resources.</li>
  <li><strong>What we write to Google Ads:</strong> declarative updates to the same resources,
      originating from <code>.bid</code> files the operator authored.</li>
  <li><strong>What we store locally:</strong> the <code>.bid</code> source files themselves (in
      Git), the environment variables listed in §7.1 (in the operator&rsquo;s local environment),
      and a small <code>.bidsmith/</code> on-disk cache of the most recent live-state read used
      to make <code>plan</code> fast. The cache contains only account configuration already
      visible to the operator; it never contains credentials.</li>
  <li><strong>What we never touch:</strong> end-user PII, third-party advertiser data, Google
      account information beyond what&rsquo;s needed for OAuth.</li>
  <li><strong>Network egress:</strong> only to <code>oauth2.googleapis.com</code> and
      <code>googleads.googleapis.com</code> over HTTPS.</li>
</ul>

<h2>9. Safety properties and audit trail</h2>

<div class="callout">
The single most important property of {{ tool_name }} is that <strong>no Google Ads resource is
ever mutated without an explicit, interactive operator confirmation</strong> — or, in the narrow
case of a CI invocation, without an explicit <code>--auto-approve</code> flag on a trusted,
non-TTY environment.
</div>

<h3>9.1 Plan-before-apply</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Every <code>apply</code> internally runs the same prepare stage as <code>plan</code>:
      parse &rarr; import &rarr; fetch live &rarr; diff &rarr; validateOnly mutate.</li>
  <li>The validateOnly result is printed in full before any real mutate is attempted.</li>
  <li>If validateOnly rejects any operation, the real mutate is <strong>skipped</strong> —
      partial application is not possible.</li>
  <li>The real mutate is gated on the operator typing literally <code>yes</code> at the prompt.
      Any other input (including <code>y</code>, <code>Y</code>, <code>YES</code>) cancels.</li>
  <li><code>apply</code> refuses to prompt when stdin is not a TTY; in that case
      <code>--auto-approve</code> must be passed explicitly.</li>
</ul>

<h3>9.2 Atomicity</h3>

<p>
All operations in one apply go to <code>googleAds:mutate</code> as a single batch. Partial
failure is not enabled, so either every operation in the batch succeeds or none of them are
written.
</p>

<h3>9.3 Audit trail</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Every change to a <code>.bid</code> file is a Git commit reviewed in a pull request. The
      operator who applied it is recorded in shell history and in their Git checkout.</li>
  <li>The full request and response of every mutate can be dumped with <code>--verbose</code>,
      and the corresponding Google Ads change-history view records the same operations under
      the operator&rsquo;s identity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>9.4 Errors are source-mapped</h3>

<p>
Schema and parse errors render with file path, line number, and a source snippet (via the
<code>miette</code> diagnostic library), so any policy violation surfaced by Google Ads&rsquo;
validateOnly response can be traced straight back to the <code>.bid</code> line that caused it.
</p>

<h2>10. Open-source repository</h2>

<p>
The full source is published at <strong>{{ source_repo_url }}</strong> under the MIT license.
Reviewers can inspect:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>src/api/auth.rs</code> — OAuth handling, no logging of secrets.</li>
  <li><code>src/api/client.rs</code> — every HTTP request {{ tool_name }} ever makes.</li>
  <li><code>src/commands/apply.rs</code> — the prompt-before-mutate flow, exactly as described in §9.1.</li>
  <li><code>src/api/mutate.rs</code> — how diffs are converted into mutate operations.</li>
  <li><code>DECISIONS.md</code> — settled architectural choices with rationale, including the
      locked plan/apply safety model.</li>
</ul>

